etize the problem in each spatial direction. In this example the exact solution is unknown. The reference solution is computed by the ERKC-C method with Gauss collocation points (s = 3) using the constant size h = norm at final time T = 3 are presented in Figures 3 and 4. The figures confirm the theoretical analysis. Moreover , a comparison of computational cost of the ERKC methods with Gauss collocation points (s 3) is presented in Table 1. For step sizes h = with k = 3,4, 9, the ERKC-I method consistently reduces the CPU time by approximately 129 compared to the ERKC-C method, demonstrating its computational efficiency across a range of step sizes grid 2-11 step 2~k"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Figure 3: The convergence rates of ERKC-I methods (in the left panel) and ERKC-C methods (in the right panel) for (6.63) The errors are measured at T = 3 in the L%(02) norm-","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":262.33333333333337,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":69.0,"r":506.0,"t":288.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,173],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"Figure 3: The convergence rates of ERKC-I methods (in the left panel) and ERKC-C methods (in the right panel) for (6.63) The errors are measured at T = 3 in the L%(02) norm-"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":441.0,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":87.66666666666667,"r":96.33333333333333,"t":447.6666666666667},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"10"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"10","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":440.3333333333333,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":303.0,"r":311.0,"t":447.0},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"10"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Radau IIA (s-2","parent":{"$ref":"#/pictures/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":428.3333333333333,